INT. GLASS-DOMED OBSERVATORY, LUNAR FAR SIDE STATION - DAY

A great telescope sleeps under a sheet of dust. Earthlight spills through the dome. Six people sit around a folding table, plates of reconstituted eggs untouched.

COMMANDER HORATIO LINDQVIST (50s, silver temples, careful) presides. DR. AMARA OKONKWO-REYES (40s, sharp, notebook) sits at his right. TECHNICIAN BEN YAZZIE (30s) fiddles with a salt packet. MRS. CORDELIA FAIRWEATHER (60s, tailored, a visitor from the supply ship) eats nothing. ENGINEER JUNE KAWAMURA (30s) stirs her coffee. A tall man in a station jacket, STRANGER (40s, unreadable), stands just inside the door, hat in hand.

COMMANDER LINDQVIST
Mrs. Fairweather, I trust you slept well.

CORDELIA
Remarkably. I'm told the silence here is the best in the solar system.

OKONKWO-REYES
It is. Nothing carries.

YAZZIE
Not even sound. Not even, y'know, gossip.

JUNE
Ben.

YAZZIE
What? I'm only saying.

The Stranger takes a step forward. Everyone turns, then politely turns back.

CORDELIA
And who is our guest?

STRANGER
We haven't met, ma'am. I came in with the supply crew.

COMMANDER LINDQVIST
He's the new relief mechanic. Name of Pryce. Mr. Pryce, sit down.

PRYCE
Thank you, Commander.

He takes the empty chair. Okonkwo-Reyes studies him over her notebook.

OKONKWO-REYES
Mr. Pryce, forgive me. The crew manifest for last Thursday's flight lists twelve passengers. Twelve names. None of them yours.

PRYCE
I came on the second shuttle.

OKONKWO-REYES
There was no second shuttle.

Silence. June's spoon stops. Yazzie's salt packet bursts, scattering white on the table like frost.

COMMANDER LINDQVIST
Dr. Okonkwo-Reyes--

PRYCE
It's all right, Commander. She's right.

He rises. Straightens his jacket. Takes off the station badge and lays it on the table, face up.

PRYCE (CONT'D)
My name is Pryce. I'm the chairman of the board that signed off on this station. I came to see for myself whether the stories were true.

CORDELIA
(coolly)
What stories?

PRYCE
That the telescope hasn't worked since March. That the funding was diverted. That everyone at this table has been lying to Earth for six months.

Nobody moves. The earthlight, blue and enormous, slides a finger of shadow across the dome.

COMMANDER LINDQVIST
Mr. Pryce. Before you say another word, I'd ask you to look up.

Pryce looks up. Through the dome, far off, a point of light blooms and pulses. Then another. Then a ring of them, pale as pearls, arranged in a perfect circle.

PRYCE
What am I looking at?

COMMANDER LINDQVIST
The reason we stopped reporting.
